    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Making the most of a small kitchen takes a strategic blend of aesthetic choices and practical ingenuity. When floor space is limited, every design element should serve a dual purpose: enhancing the look of the room while improving how well it works. From clever storage to the way colour and light shape our sense of space, turning a compact kitchen into a stylish, efficient room is entirely achievable with the right approach.</p>

<h2 id="vertical-space">1. Vertical space</h2>
<!--figure:kitchen-open-shelving-plants|Open timber shelves keep the wall light and put everyday pieces and plants on display.-->
<p>In a small kitchen, the walls are your greatest asset. Instead of bulky upper cabinets that can make a room feel enclosed, consider open shelving. Running shelves all the way to the ceiling draws the eye upward and creates a sense of height. The top tiers provide storage for items you use less often, while the lower shelves let you display attractive cookware and glassware, turning functional pieces into decor.</p>

<h2 id="layered-lighting">2. Layered lighting</h2>
<!--figure:walnut-kitchen-island-pendants|Pendants over the island add ambient light and a focal point, alongside the room’s other light sources.-->
<p>Good lighting is one of the most effective tools for making a small space feel larger. A single overhead light often casts harsh shadows that make corners feel cramped. By layering light, with task lighting under the cabinets, ambient light from pendants and accent lighting inside glass-fronted cabinets, you remove dark spots. The result feels more expansive, and every workspace is well lit.</p>

<h2 id="monochromatic-schemes">3. Monochromatic schemes</h2>
<!--figure:white-handleless-kitchen|White handleless cabinets, a pale marble splashback and a matching island keep the palette calm and continuous.-->
<p>A monochromatic palette, particularly in light shades such as white, cream or pale grey, reduces visual “noise”. When the cabinets, walls and worktops blend together, the edges of the room feel less defined and the space reads as larger. To add depth without breaking the colour flow, play with texture: combine matte cabinet fronts with a glossy tiled splashback to keep the look lively yet unified.</p>

<h2 id="pull-out-pantries">4. Pull-out pantries</h2>
<p>Hidden storage is essential for keeping a small kitchen clutter-free. Slim pull-out pantries can be fitted into the “dead space” often found next to appliances or at the end of a run of cabinets. These narrow units hold a surprising amount while staying easy to reach. Keeping spices, oils and dry goods tucked away frees up worktop space, the most precious commodity in a small kitchen.</p>

<h2 id="mirrored-splashbacks">5. Mirrored splashbacks</h2>
<p>Mirrors are a classic interior design trick for expanding space, and they work well in the kitchen too. A mirrored splashback reflects light and movement, visually “doubling” the depth of the worktop. If a full mirror feels too bold, consider tinted glass or high-gloss subway tiles with a metallic finish. These surfaces bounce light around the room, making even a narrow galley kitchen feel bright and airy.</p>

<h2 id="fold-down-tables">6. Fold-down tables</h2>
<p>When a permanent dining table isn’t an option, a wall-mounted fold-down table offers a versatile solution. It can serve as extra prep space while you cook and a place to eat when needed, then fold flat against the wall once the meal is over. This keeps a clear route through the kitchen and avoids the boxed-in feeling that permanent furniture can create in tight quarters.</p>

<h2 id="ceiling-racks">7. Ceiling racks</h2>
<p>A hanging pot rack is a functional decor element that frees up a good deal of cabinet space. Hanging bulky items such as frying pans, colanders and saucepans from the ceiling makes use of the overhead volume that usually goes ignored. Your most-used tools stay within easy reach, and the rack adds a professional, “chef’s kitchen” look.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Position the rack over an island or a spot outside the main walkway, and have it fixed securely into a ceiling joist, since a fully loaded rack is heavy.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="herb-gardens">8. Herb gardens</h2>
<p>Bringing greenery into a small kitchen adds life and a pop of natural colour without taking up much room. A windowsill herb garden or a vertical wall planter provides fresh ingredients for cooking while acting as a living piece of art. Plants soften the hard lines of appliances and cabinetry, making the kitchen feel lived-in rather than purely practical.</p>

<h2 id="patterned-runners">9. Patterned runners</h2>
<p>The floor is often overlooked in kitchen decorating. A long, patterned runner can define the space and add comfort and style. In a small kitchen, a runner with a linear pattern draws the eye along the length of the room, making it feel longer. Rugs are also an excellent way to introduce colour and personality into a rented kitchen where you may not be able to change the cabinets or splashback.</p>

<h2 id="minimalist-essentials">10. Minimalist essentials</h2>
<p>The golden rule of small kitchen decorating is “less is more”. To keep the space feeling intentional and sophisticated, take a minimalist approach to worktop decor. Choose a few high-quality, beautiful pieces to display, such as a designer kettle, a sculptural fruit bowl or a set of wooden spoons in a ceramic crock, and hide the rest. Reducing visual clutter is the fastest way to make a small kitchen feel high-end and spacious.</p>

